# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# Every runtime setting of aicc-knowledge. This file is the registry of KB_*
# keys: internal/config/config_test.go fails when it and the config package
# disagree.
#
#   cp .env.example .env   # then uncomment only what this deployment changes
#
# The service reads .env from its working directory at startup and never
# overrides a real environment variable with it. Lines beginning with # are
# ignored; surrounding single or double quotes are stripped from values. There
# is no inline-comment syntax: everything after the first = is the value, so
# keep notes on their own line.
#
# An empty value means "unset". Service URLs have no default in code. Each
# command names what it needs and refuses to start without it.

#
# Process
#
# KB_ENV must be dev or prod. dev logs as text, prod logs JSON.
#KB_ENV=dev
# One of debug, info, warn, error.
#KB_LOG_LEVEL=info
# Service name reported to OpenTelemetry.
#KB_SERVICE_NAME=aicc-knowledge

#
# HTTP
#
# The authenticated API (/v1/...).
#KB_HTTP_ADDR=:8080
# The unauthenticated ops listener: /metrics, /healthz, /readyz. Keep it off
# public networks.
#KB_OPS_ADDR=127.0.0.1:9090
# Per-dependency timeout of one /readyz check, in milliseconds.
#KB_READYZ_TIMEOUT_MS=2000

#
# Database - PostgreSQL 18, the source of truth. Migrations run at startup.
# Required by serve and every command that touches the database.
#
#KB_DATABASE_URL=
# Pool size. Must be >= 1.
#KB_DATABASE_MAX_CONNS=10

#
# Meilisearch - derived, rebuildable index. Required by serve.
#
#KB_MEILI_URL=
# Master or admin key. Leave empty when the instance runs without one.
#KB_MEILI_API_KEY=
# Live index uids are this prefix plus the language: faq_en and faq_zh. Change
# it only to share one Meilisearch between deployments (staging and production).
#KB_MEILI_INDEX_PREFIX=faq_

#
# TEI - text embeddings inference serving BAAI/bge-m3. Required by serve.
#
#KB_TEI_URL=
# Optional second TEI for offline document embedding (publish, rollback).
# TEI serves requests in order on CPU, so a long publish batch queues in front
# of query embeddings on a shared instance. Defaults to KB_TEI_URL.
#KB_TEI_BATCH_URL=
# Vector size of the embedder. bge-m3 is 1024. The search indexes are created
# with this size, and publish refuses a TEI that answers another size.
#KB_EMBEDDING_DIMENSIONS=1024

#
# S3 - where the source files live. AWS, MinIO and SeaweedFS all work.
#
# Required by scan and parse: KB_S3_BUCKET. Credentials left empty fall back to the AWS
# default credential chain.
#
# Leave the endpoint empty for AWS. Set it to the base URL of MinIO or SeaweedFS.
#KB_S3_ENDPOINT=
#KB_S3_REGION=us-east-1
#KB_S3_BUCKET=
# Only keys under this prefix are scanned.
#KB_S3_PREFIX=
# MinIO and SeaweedFS need path-style addressing.
#KB_S3_USE_PATH_STYLE=true
#KB_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
#KB_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Objects larger than this many bytes are still hashed (streamed, never held
# in memory) and versioned, but as UNSUPPORTED with parse_error_code
# OBJECT_TOO_LARGE and no parse job. Parsers hold a file in memory, so this
# bounds them. Default 50 MiB.
#KB_S3_MAX_OBJECT_BYTES=52428800
# Derives each document's search scope from the S3 key of its source file.
# Placeholders name leading directories below KB_S3_PREFIX, in order, and each
# must be a key of KB_SEARCH_SCOPE_KEYS. With "{brand}/{channel}", the object
# acme/web/faq.docx gives scope brand=acme, channel=web. A key with fewer
# directories leaves the remaining keys unset, and a document without a scope
# key is global for it: a search that filters on brand=globex still returns
# it. Empty means no document has a scope. The scope is snapshotted when a
# publication is built.
#KB_S3_SCOPE_PATH_TEMPLATE=

#
# Search
#
# Comma-separated scope keys a caller may filter on. Any other key in a search
# request is rejected with UNKNOWN_SCOPE_KEY.
#KB_SEARCH_SCOPE_KEYS=
# NO_MATCH threshold per index. A vector score is (1+cosine)/2, so unrelated
# content scores 0.5 and the value must be in (0.5, 1]. The defaults are
# starting points, not guarantees: EN 0.85 comes from one eval --sweep on a
# small synthetic sample, raised because a wrong spoken answer costs more than
# a NO_MATCH handed to a person. ZH 0.875 was calibrated offline on a private
# real sample of 170 questions (no data is in this repository) together with
# the product guard below; see docs/research/README.md, caveat C4.
# Re-calibrate on your own questions with `aicc-knowledge eval --sweep`.
#KB_SEARCH_THRESHOLD_EN=0.85
#KB_SEARCH_THRESHOLD_ZH=0.875
# Product guard (active only for a language whose live publication was built
# with a product catalog, see README "Product catalog"). A hit that is about no
# particular product ("generic") counts as a HIT only from threshold plus this
# margin; generic FAQs otherwise match off-topic questions. In [0, 0.5). The EN
# default is 0 because there is no real EN data to calibrate on yet.
#KB_SEARCH_GENERIC_MARGIN_EN=0
#KB_SEARCH_GENERIC_MARGIN_ZH=0.04
# How often serve reloads the product catalog of each language's live
# publication, in seconds. After a publish or rollback that changes the
# catalog, queries are read with the old catalog for up to this long.
#KB_PRODUCTS_REFRESH_SEC=30
# Request budget when the caller sends no timeoutMs, in milliseconds.
#KB_SEARCH_DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
# Upper bound for a caller's timeoutMs. At most 60000.
#KB_SEARCH_MAX_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

#
# Publish - building and swapping the Meilisearch indexes.
#
# How many SUPERSEDED publications keep their Meilisearch index, newest first,
# so a rollback is one swap. Older ones lose the index (publication rows and
# items are kept forever) and a rollback to them rebuilds it. 0 keeps none.
#KB_PUBLISH_RETAIN_INDEXES=3

#
# Facts
#
# IANA time zone whose current date is the default `at` of
# POST /v1/facts/{table}/lookup when the caller sends none. Needed because a
# price valid "today" depends on where the business is. Examples: UTC,
# Asia/Shanghai.
#KB_TIMEZONE=UTC

#
# LLM - offline candidate generation through an OpenAI-compatible endpoint.
#
# Used by generate. Any server that speaks POST {base}/chat/completions with
# response_format json_schema works (OpenAI, DeepSeek, vLLM, llama-server).
# The base URL includes the version path, for example http://host:8080/v1.
#KB_LLM_BASE_URL=
# Sent as a Bearer token only when set; local servers need none.
#KB_LLM_API_KEY=
#KB_LLM_MODEL=
# Timeout of one HTTP attempt to the LLM, in milliseconds. A failed attempt
# (timeout, 429, 5xx) is retried twice with backoff.
#KB_LLM_TIMEOUT_MS=120000
# 0 makes generation as repeatable as the server allows.
#KB_LLM_TEMPERATURE=0
# Optional integer sent as `seed`; with temperature 0 it makes runs repeatable.
#KB_LLM_SEED=

#
# Generate - limits applied to every LLM-written candidate.
#
# Longest spoken answer, in characters. Answers are read out by TTS, so they
# stay short: about two sentences. Chinese carries more per character.
#KB_GENERATE_MAX_ANSWER_CHARS_EN=240
#KB_GENERATE_MAX_ANSWER_CHARS_ZH=90

#
# OpenTelemetry
#
# OTLP/HTTP trace endpoint. Empty turns tracing off.
#KB_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
